<h2 id="ownership">Own the whole task, including the part nobody sees</h2>
<p>“I will cook if you tell me what to make” leaves the planning with her. So does doing the laundry but leaving a pile she has to sort before anyone has clean clothes.</p>
<p>Pick an area you can handle from beginning to end. With meals, that means finding out what sounds tolerable, checking the fridge, buying the ingredients, cooking, and cleaning up. With appointments, it might mean keeping a shared calendar, arranging transport if she wants it, and making space in your workday.</p>
<p>Agree on the task once. Then remember it. A reminder on your own phone is a perfectly respectable piece of parenting equipment.</p>
<ul><li><strong>Instead of:</strong> “Let me know if you need anything.” <strong>Try:</strong> “I can handle dinner and the kitchen tonight. Does soup sound okay, or would something plain be better?”</li>
<li><strong>Instead of:</strong> “You should get more rest.” <strong>Try:</strong> “I have the dishes and the dog. Go lie down.”</li>
<li><strong>Instead of:</strong> “What else is on the baby list?” <strong>Try:</strong> “I will check our leave paperwork and tell you what I find.”</li></ul>
<p>Taking ownership does not mean deciding everything for her. Her body, appointments, and birth preferences are hers. Your job is to make your shared life easier to carry, with her input where it matters.</p>
<h2 id="listen">Ask whether she wants listening, help, or company</h2>
<p>If she says she feels miserable, you do not have to find a bright side before the sentence is over. “At least the baby is healthy” can shut down a conversation she needed to have. So can explaining that pregnancy is supposed to be hard.</p>
<p>Try something smaller: “That sounds exhausting. Do you want me to listen, help figure something out, or just sit with you?” Then believe the answer. If she wants to vent, put the phone down and let the conversation belong to her for a few minutes.</p>
<p>Skip the hormones explanation. Even when pregnancy changes how someone feels, that does not make their experience less real. And affection should not come with an expectation of sex. Ask what feels comfortable now, and accept that the answer may change.</p>
<p>You will get this wrong sometimes. When you do, make the repair specific: “I jumped into advice when you needed me to hear you. I am sorry. Tell me again.” That gets you further than a ten minute explanation of your intentions.</p>
<h2>Listen to the women in the conversation</h2>
<p>In <a href="/podcast#our-wives-get-honest-about-pregnancy-postpartum-us">Our Wives Get Honest About Pregnancy, Postpartum &amp; Us</a>, Vivian and Abigail join John and Michael to talk about pregnancy, postpartum, marriage, careers, community, and support from a husband. It is useful listening precisely because the conversation includes the people receiving that support.</p>
<p>Listen together if she wants to. Afterward, ask one question: “What would you want me to understand about your experience?” Leave room for an answer that surprises you.</p>
<BlogOffer heading="Hear the conversation together" body="Vivian and Abigail join the podcast for an honest conversation about pregnancy, postpartum, and marriage." href="/podcast#our-wives-get-honest-about-pregnancy-postpartum-us" label="Listen to the wives episode" />

<h2 id="trimester">What support looks like in each trimester</h2>
<h3>First trimester: believe the tiredness you cannot see</h3>
<p>There may be no obvious bump and still be a huge change in her daily capacity. Do not use how she looks as the measure of how she feels. Ask about food smells, errands, and plans that have become harder. Offer to cancel or simplify something instead of insisting she keep up.</p>
<p>Ask which appointments she would like you to attend. Keep your own notes and questions so she does not have to teach you everything afterward. If you cannot be there, make time to hear how it went without turning it into a rushed status report.</p>
<h3>Second trimester: turn the breathing room into shared preparation</h3>
<p>Some people feel better during this stretch. Some do not. Either way, you can start the conversations that are easy to avoid: leave from work, childcare, the budget, and who can help after the birth.</p>
<p>Choose a childbirth class together and put it on your calendar. For the money conversation, use our <a href="/blog/cost-of-a-baby">baby cost guide</a> to identify the categories you need to price for your own household. Come with information, including your employer’s actual leave policy, rather than another vague “we should look into that.”</p>
<h3>Third trimester: reduce the number of loose ends</h3>
<p>Check your route to the birth location and the instructions for arriving after hours. Pack from the <a href="/hospital-bag-checklist">hospital bag checklist</a>. Discuss the <a href="/birth-plan-template">birth plan</a>, including how she wants you to help if circumstances change.</p>
<p>Then look past the birth itself. Who handles groceries when you get home? Who tells visitors it is time to leave? Where are the recovery instructions going to live so both of you can find them? Write the answers somewhere shared. A plan neither person can find at midnight is mostly decoration.</p>
