<h2>Make rest possible, including after the baby arrives</h2>
<p>Rest needs coverage. If she lies down but still has to answer questions about dinner, guests, and laundry, she is still on duty. Give her a stretch of time when those decisions belong to you.</p>
<p>Before the birth, ask who she would actually feel comfortable accepting help from. A person who brings food and takes out the trash can be useful. A visitor who needs entertaining can add work. Agree on boundaries together, then communicate them to your own family yourself.</p>
<p>Talk about nights before you are both exhausted. Feeding needs and recovery will shape the plan, so keep it flexible. You can handle a diaper change, bring water, clean feeding equipment according to its instructions, or settle the baby after a feed. “I cannot breastfeed” does not use up the list of available jobs.</p>
<BlogOffer heading="Want a structured resource for baby sleep?" body="Explore Taking Cara Babies for guidance suited to your baby’s stage. Check the program’s age range and follow your clinician’s feeding and medical advice." href="https://www.takingcarababies.com?sca_ref=11694397.hvn2tySyLaykC" label="Explore Taking Cara Babies" affiliate />

<h2>Know when the helpful move is calling the care team</h2>
<p>You do not need to decide whether a symptom is “normal” from an internet search. Keep her pregnancy care team’s number and after hours instructions handy. If she is worried about a symptom, help her contact them and describe what is happening. For an emergency, call emergency services.</p>
<p>For general preparation, the <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/best-start-in-life/pregnancy/advice-for-partners/">NHS advice for partners during pregnancy</a> covers classes, household preparation, and practical support. Her own clinicians are the right people for questions about her individual pregnancy.</p>
<h2 id="tonight">A useful plan for tonight</h2>
<p>Keep it simple enough to actually do. Ask what is taking the most out of her this week. Listen all the way through. Choose one recurring responsibility you can own, and agree on what “handled” looks like. Put the next appointment or preparation task on your own calendar.</p>
<p>Then do the task tomorrow without needing another conversation about it. That is where trust gets built.</p>
